// ed-principal — generated from src/ed-principal-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// School Principal / Administrator. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["ed-principal"] = (QB["ed-principal"] || []).concat([
["Leadership", "What is your leadership philosophy as a principal?", "**Why they ask:** vision and how you lead adults set the tone for the whole school.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I see the principal's core job as instructional leadership — everything else serves student learning. I lead through a clear shared vision, trust in my teachers as professionals, and visible presence in classrooms. I distribute leadership so teacher leaders own improvement rather than waiting on me, and I make decisions transparently against what's best for kids. I'd rather develop my staff than control them, but I hold high standards and address problems directly.\"\n\nTip: Center instructional leadership and developing adults.", "B", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does instructional leadership look like day to day?","Regular classroom walkthroughs with feedback, protecting teachers' planning time, leading data conversations, and keeping meetings focused on teaching and learning."]}],
["Achievement", "How would you improve student achievement at a school?", "**Why they ask:** raising outcomes is the central accountability measure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start by understanding the data and the people — which students and subgroups are underserved, what's working, and what teachers need. Then I focus on a few high-leverage priorities rather than a dozen initiatives: strong first instruction, a system of intervention for students who fall behind, and real collaboration time for teacher teams to look at student work. I set measurable goals, monitor progress frequently, and adjust. Sustained focus on a few things beats initiative overload.\"\n\nTip: Diagnose first, then focus on a few high-leverage priorities.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you avoid initiative overload?","Pick two or three priorities, say no to the rest for now, and protect them — schools fail more from too many initiatives than too few."]}],
["Culture", "How do you build a positive school culture and retain good teachers?", "**Why they ask:** culture and retention drive everything else, and turnover is costly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Teachers stay where they feel supported, trusted and able to grow. I build culture by being fair and consistent, protecting teachers from unnecessary burdens, recognizing good work specifically, and backing them with students and parents when they're in the right. I invest in meaningful professional development and give teachers real voice in decisions. For students, I set a predictable, respectful environment where staff build relationships. Culture is built in hundreds of small, consistent actions.\"\n\nTip: Support, trust and voice retain teachers more than perks.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Why do teachers usually leave a school?","Often not pay but feeling unsupported by leadership, overwhelmed, or powerless — addressing those is within a principal's control."]}],
["Staff management", "How do you handle an underperforming teacher?", "**Why they ask:** staff supervision and the courage to address problems are essential.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with clarity and support, not punishment: observe to get specifics, name the gap directly and privately, and offer coaching, modeling or mentoring with a clear timeline and measurable expectations. Most teachers improve with good support. If they don't, I follow the formal evaluation and improvement process, document carefully, and involve HR, because fairness and due process protect everyone including students. I don't let a persistent problem slide — kids only get one year with that teacher.\"\n\nTip: Support and document; follow due process but don't avoid it.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What if the teacher has tenure or union protection?","The same standards apply — I document objectively, follow the contractual process precisely, and partner with HR; protections require fair process, not inaction."]}],
["Operations", "How do you manage a school budget and resources?", "**Why they ask:** principals steward significant public funds.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I align spending to the school's instructional priorities and student needs, not to whatever's traditional. I involve staff in decisions so the budget reflects real classroom needs, track categorical and grant funds carefully because they have compliance rules, and look for the highest-impact use of limited dollars — often that's staffing and teacher development over programs or materials. I'm transparent about trade-offs with staff so people understand why a request was or wasn't funded.\"\n\nTip: Spend against instructional priorities and respect fund restrictions.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you handle restricted categorical or grant funds?","Spend them only on allowable purposes, document it, and track separately — misusing restricted funds creates audit and legal risk."]}],
["Safety", "How do you approach student discipline and school safety?", "**Why they ask:** safety and fair discipline are legal and moral responsibilities.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I want discipline to be fair, consistent and focused on changing behavior, not just excluding students — I lean toward restorative and support-based approaches while keeping clear consequences and keeping everyone safe. I follow due process and the ed code, especially for suspensions and for students with IEPs, who have additional protections. On physical safety I make sure we have, practice and review emergency plans, and I watch discipline data by subgroup to catch disproportionate patterns.\"\n\nTip: Fair, consistent, restorative where possible, and legally sound.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why watch discipline data by subgroup?","Disproportionate suspension of particular groups is both an equity problem and a compliance risk — you can't fix what you don't monitor."]}],
["Community", "How do you engage families and the community?", "**Why they ask:** family partnership and community trust support the school.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat families as partners and make the school welcoming and communicative — regular, clear, two-way communication in families' languages, not just one-way announcements. I create real avenues for input, show up at community events, and respond to concerns promptly and respectfully. When families trust the school, attendance, behavior and achievement all benefit. I'm especially intentional about reaching families who've historically felt unwelcome in schools.\"\n\nTip: Two-way, multilingual communication and genuine welcome.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle a vocal, upset parent group?","Listen openly, acknowledge valid concerns, share the facts and constraints honestly, and involve them in part of the solution rather than getting defensive."]}],
["Equity", "How do you address achievement gaps between student groups?", "**Why they ask:** closing gaps is a core equity and accountability expectation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I make the gaps visible by disaggregating data, because averages hide them. Then I look for root causes — access to rigorous courses, quality of instruction, discipline patterns, support systems — and address the ones in our control. That often means ensuring underserved students get the strongest teachers and access to advanced coursework, targeted support, and high expectations rather than remediation that keeps them behind. I hold the whole staff accountable for every subgroup, not just the overall number.\"\n\nTip: Make gaps visible, then fix access and expectations, not just 'fix the kids.'", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why can remediation sometimes widen gaps?","If struggling students only get slower, lower-level work they never catch up — access to grade-level content with support closes gaps better."]}],
["Scenario", "You take over a school with low morale and declining scores. What are your first 90 days?", "**Why they ask:** turnaround judgment and pacing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd spend the first weeks listening — in classrooms, with staff, students and families — and studying the data before changing much, because acting before understanding erodes trust. I'd identify a few root causes and quick wins to build momentum and credibility, stabilize anything unsafe or broken immediately, and name two or three clear priorities. Then I'd build the teacher teams and routines to carry the work. Real improvement is multi-year, but the first 90 days are about trust, diagnosis and focus.\"\n\nTip: Listen and diagnose first; quick wins build credibility for the long work.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not make big changes immediately?","Changing things before you understand them signals you don't value the staff's knowledge and usually breaks something that was working."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a difficult decision you had to make as a leader.", "**Why they ask:** real leadership experience and values under pressure.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"As an assistant principal I had to recommend non-renewal for a well-liked teacher whose instruction wasn't improving despite a year of support. It was personally hard because colleagues liked him, but students were falling behind. I made sure the process was fair and documented, had the honest conversation directly rather than hiding behind HR, and offered to help him find a better-fit role. I learned that protecting students sometimes means an unpopular, humane decision made the right way.\"\n\nTip: Show you'll make the hard, student-centered call fairly.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you keep staff trust after an unpopular decision?","Be transparent about the reasoning within what's confidential, stay consistent, and show the decision was about students and made fairly."]}],
["Governance", "How do you work with your superintendent, district, and school board?", "**Why they ask:** principals operate within a larger accountability structure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep my supervisor informed with no surprises — especially on anything political, legal or safety-related — and bring problems with a proposed solution, not just the problem. I implement district initiatives faithfully while advocating honestly for my school's needs and giving candid feedback upward. I understand the board sets policy and the superintendent runs operations, and I stay in my lane while being a reliable, trustworthy member of the leadership team.\"\n\nTip: No surprises upward; advocate honestly within the structure.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do if you disagree with a district directive?","Raise my concerns candidly and privately with evidence; if it stands and it's legal and ethical, I implement it well and loyally."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want to be a principal, and what kind of leader will you be?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and self-awareness for a demanding role.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I moved into leadership because as a teacher I could change outcomes for my students, but as a principal I can change conditions for a whole school's worth of students and support the teachers who do that work daily. I'll be a leader who's present in classrooms, honest, developmental with staff, and relentless about equity — someone teachers trust and students feel safe with. The job is hard, but shaping the conditions for learning at scale is exactly the work I want.\"\n\nTip: Connect the move to broader impact and name your concrete leadership traits.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you sustain yourself in such a demanding role?","Protect time for the instructional core, delegate and build leaders so it's not all on me, and keep boundaries so I can last for the multi-year work."]}]
]);

// School Principal / Administrator — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["ed-principal"] = (QB["ed-principal"] || []).concat([
["Hiring", "How do you hire and build a strong teaching staff?", "**Why they ask:** hiring is one of a principal's highest-leverage levers.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hiring is the most important thing I do — one great hire affects students for years. I look for evidence of impact on student learning, a growth mindset and coachability, cultural fit with our mission, and genuine care for kids, using structured interviews, a demonstration lesson, and reference checks rather than gut feel. I involve teachers in the process so the team owns the hire. And I hire for the long game: potential and values over just polish, because skills can be coached.\"\n\nTip: Structured process, demo lesson, values and coachability over polish.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why include a demonstration lesson?","It shows how a candidate actually teaches and interacts with students, which an interview answer can't reveal."]}],
["Supervision", "How do you give teachers useful feedback from classroom observations?", "**Why they ask:** instructional feedback drives teacher growth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'm in classrooms frequently with short, frequent walkthroughs, not just the annual formal observation, so feedback is ongoing and low-stakes. I keep it specific, evidence-based and focused on one or two high-leverage moves rather than a laundry list, and I frame it as coaching toward a goal the teacher owns. I ask reflective questions before giving my take, and I follow up to see whether it changed practice. The point is better teaching, not catching people.\"\n\nTip: Frequent, specific, coaching-oriented, one or two things at a time.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How is a walkthrough different from a formal evaluation?","Walkthroughs are short, frequent, low-stakes check-ins for coaching; formal evaluations are the longer, documented process for accountability."]}],
["Development", "How do you plan professional development that teachers actually value?", "**Why they ask:** most PD is wasted; good leaders make it count.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I tie PD to a few clear instructional priorities and to real needs identified from data and teacher input, not one-off trendy workshops. I favor job-embedded learning — coaching, teacher teams analyzing student work, peer observation — over sit-and-get, and I give teachers voice and some choice. I protect time for it and follow up so it changes practice rather than evaporating. Teachers value PD that respects their expertise and clearly helps their students.\"\n\nTip: Focused, job-embedded, teacher-informed PD tied to priorities.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Why is job-embedded PD more effective?","It happens in the real context of teaching with follow-through, so it changes daily practice instead of being a forgotten workshop."]}],
["Operations", "How do you approach building the master schedule?", "**Why they ask:** the schedule encodes a school's priorities and constraints.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The master schedule is a values document — it decides who gets access to what, how much intervention time exists, and whether teachers get real collaboration time. I build it backward from priorities: protected common planning for teacher teams, intervention and enrichment blocks, and equitable access to advanced courses. I balance that against staffing, contracts and student needs, and I involve staff so it's realistic. A thoughtful schedule quietly enables most of our instructional goals.\"\n\nTip: Schedule from priorities — collaboration time, intervention, equitable access.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Why is the schedule an equity issue?","Because it determines which students can access advanced courses and support — scheduling choices can quietly widen or close opportunity gaps."]}],
["Systems", "How do you use a multi-tiered system of support (MTSS) or RTI?", "**Why they ask:** tiered support is standard practice for meeting student needs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"MTSS gives every student strong core instruction (Tier 1), adds targeted small-group support for those who need more (Tier 2), and intensive individualized support for the few who need the most (Tier 3), for both academics and behavior. I make sure we use data to identify needs early, match the intervention to the need, monitor whether it's working, and adjust — rather than waiting for failure. It also feeds appropriate referrals for evaluation. Strong Tier 1 is what keeps the upper tiers manageable.\"\n\nTip: Strong core instruction plus data-driven, monitored tiers of support.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why invest most in Tier 1?","If core instruction is strong, far fewer students need intensive support — weak Tier 1 floods the upper tiers and overwhelms resources."]}],
["Compliance", "How do you ensure special education compliance and good outcomes?", "**Why they ask:** IDEA compliance carries legal and ethical weight.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure IEPs are followed, timelines and meetings happen, least-restrictive-environment decisions are driven by student need, and procedural safeguards and parent rights are honored — non-compliance creates real legal exposure and, more importantly, fails kids. Beyond compliance I push for genuine inclusion and strong outcomes: that gen-ed and special-ed teachers co-plan, that accommodations actually happen in classrooms, and that we watch the data. I lean on my special-ed staff's expertise and keep learning the law myself.\"\n\nTip: Compliance and real inclusion — follow the law and watch outcomes.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's a common special-ed compliance failure?","Missed timelines or accommodations not actually implemented in classrooms — both are legal issues and both are preventable with good systems."]}],
["Strategy", "How do you develop and communicate a vision for the school?", "**Why they ask:** vision aligns a whole community.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I develop vision with the community, not just for it — listening to staff, families and students so it reflects shared values and real needs, then articulating it clearly and tying every initiative back to it. I communicate it constantly and in concrete terms, model it myself, and build it into hiring, the schedule and PD so it's lived, not laminated. A vision only matters if people can see it in daily decisions and their own work.\"\n\nTip: Co-create it, connect every decision to it, and model it daily.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you keep a vision from becoming an empty slogan?","Tie concrete decisions and resources to it, revisit it in real choices, and hold yourself accountable to it visibly."]}],
["Change", "How do you lead change when staff are resistant?", "**Why they ask:** change management is central to school improvement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I take resistance as information, not defiance — often it's reasonable fear, change fatigue, or a flaw in my plan I should hear. I build the case with data and the 'why,' involve staff in shaping the how so they own it, start with willing early adopters and quick wins, and provide real support and time. I'm honest about what's non-negotiable and what's open. I move at a sustainable pace but don't let resistance veto change that students need.\"\n\nTip: Treat resistance as information; build the why, involve staff, support the change.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What do you do with a staff member who actively undermines a change?","Address it directly and privately — understand the concern, set clear expectations, and if it continues, treat it as a performance and professionalism issue."]}],
["Crisis", "How do you handle a safety crisis or emergency at your school?", "**Why they ask:** student and staff safety is the top responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In the moment I follow our emergency protocols, stay calm and decisive, and prioritize life safety and clear communication with staff and first responders. Before a crisis, I make sure we have current plans, that staff and students know and have practiced them through drills, and that we've built relationships with local responders. Afterward I provide support and counseling, communicate honestly with families, and debrief to improve. Preparation is what makes calm, effective response possible.\"\n\nTip: Prepare and drill beforehand; stay calm and follow protocol in the moment.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you communicate with families during a crisis?","Quickly, honestly and through official channels — accurate information prevents panic and rumor, even when I can't yet share everything."]}],
["Communications", "A negative story about your school spreads on social media. How do you respond?", "**Why they ask:** reputation management and composure under scrutiny.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I stay calm, get the facts quickly, and coordinate with my district's communications guidance before responding, because a rushed or defensive reaction makes it worse. I communicate honestly and promptly with my community through official channels, acknowledge legitimate concerns, protect student and staff privacy, and focus on what we're doing to address it. I don't argue with people online. The goal is to be transparent and trustworthy, and to let actions, not spin, rebuild confidence.\"\n\nTip: Facts first, coordinate with district, honest official communication, protect privacy.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why not just respond immediately to set the record straight?","Rushing risks errors, privacy violations and escalation — getting the facts and coordinating first protects the response's credibility."]}],
["Governance", "How do you handle a politically sensitive situation involving the board or community?", "**Why they ask:** principals navigate real political pressure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep my supervisor informed early so there are no surprises, stay grounded in what's best and legal for students, and communicate with facts and professionalism rather than taking sides in the politics. I listen to all stakeholders respectfully, am transparent about constraints, and make decisions I can defend on the merits. I understand the governance roles — board sets policy, administration implements — and I stay in my lane while advocating honestly for my school.\"\n\nTip: No surprises upward, decisions grounded in student interest and law.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What if community pressure conflicts with what's best for students?","I listen genuinely, explain the reasoning and constraints, and make the defensible call for students — while keeping my supervisor informed."]}],
["Labor", "How do you work within a union contract and maintain good labor relations?", "**Why they ask:** most principals manage within collective bargaining agreements.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I know the contract and follow it precisely — it sets the rules we both agreed to — and I build a relationship of trust and fairness with staff and the association so issues get solved before they become grievances. I'm consistent and transparent, address problems directly and respectfully, and use the proper process for performance issues. Good labor relations come from treating people fairly and honestly every day, not just from the contract language.\"\n\nTip: Know and follow the contract; build trust so issues resolve early.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you address a performance problem under a strong contract?","Follow the contractual evaluation and improvement process precisely, document objectively, and partner with HR — due process and inaction are not the same thing."]}],
["Leadership", "How do you distribute leadership and develop your assistant principals and teacher leaders?", "**Why they ask:** no principal can do it alone, and succession matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I distribute real authority, not just tasks — teacher leaders own instructional initiatives, APs lead meaningful areas — because that builds capacity and ownership. I'm deliberate about developing people: giving them stretch opportunities, coaching, and honest feedback, and growing future leaders intentionally so the school's success doesn't depend on me alone. A strong, developed team lets me focus on the highest-leverage work and makes the school resilient.\"\n\nTip: Distribute real authority and intentionally grow the next leaders.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Why develop leaders who might leave for their own principalship?","Because growing leaders strengthens the school now and the system overall — and a reputation for developing people attracts strong staff."]}],
["Student support", "How do you build systems for student mental health and wellbeing?", "**Why they ask:** student mental health needs have grown sharply.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I build a tiered system: a positive schoolwide climate and SEL for all students, counselors and support staff with manageable caseloads for targeted help, and clear protocols and community partnerships for students in crisis or needing clinical care. I make sure staff know how to notice and refer, and that we respond to needs rather than only to behavior. I advocate for adequate counseling staffing because it's often stretched far too thin, and I protect student privacy throughout.\"\n\nTip: Whole-school climate, adequate counseling, clear crisis protocols and partnerships.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do when counseling needs exceed your staff capacity?","Triage by urgency, build community referral partnerships, and advocate hard to leadership for the staffing the data shows we need."]}],
["Attendance", "How do you address chronic absenteeism?", "**Why they ask:** attendance strongly predicts outcomes and is a common challenge.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat chronic absenteeism as a symptom and look for causes — illness, transportation, mental health, disengagement, family circumstances — then address them rather than just penalizing. I use early data to catch at-risk students, build relationships and reasons to come to school, remove barriers where we can, and partner with families and support services. I set a welcoming climate, because students come to places where they feel they belong and are learning something worthwhile.\"\n\nTip: Diagnose causes, remove barriers, build belonging — not just punish.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What counts as chronic absenteeism?","Typically missing about 10% or more of school days for any reason — it's a strong early warning sign worth monitoring and acting on."]}],
["Discipline", "How do you implement restorative or positive behavior approaches schoolwide?", "**Why they ask:** discipline reform is a major leadership area.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I set clear, consistent expectations and teach behavior like we teach academics (PBIS), and I use restorative practices to repair harm and keep students in school learning rather than defaulting to exclusion that often makes things worse. I invest in staff training and buy-in, because these approaches fail if teachers see them as 'no consequences' — they're not; they hold students accountable differently. I monitor discipline data by subgroup to ensure fairness and keep everyone safe.\"\n\nTip: Teach expectations, repair harm, train staff, watch the data.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you respond to staff who think restorative means no consequences?","Clarify that it holds students accountable and repairs harm — it's not permissiveness — and show the data on exclusion's poor outcomes."]}],
["Family engagement", "How do you build partnerships with community organizations?", "**Why they ask:** schools can't meet every need alone.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I map the needs our students and families have beyond academics — health, food, mentoring, enrichment — and partner with community organizations, businesses and agencies that can help, sometimes as a community-schools model. I make the partnerships genuine and coordinated rather than scattered, with clear roles and shared goals, and I protect instructional focus so partnerships serve learning. Strong community ties bring resources and trust that make the school stronger.\"\n\nTip: Match partnerships to real needs; keep them coordinated and learning-focused.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's a community-schools model?","A school that integrates academics with health, social services and family supports through partnerships, treating the school as a community hub."]}],
["Climate", "How do you measure and improve school climate?", "**Why they ask:** climate underlies learning, behavior and retention.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I measure it with surveys of students, staff and families, plus data like attendance, discipline and retention, and I actually share and act on the results rather than filing them. I look for whether people feel safe, respected and connected, and I target the specific gaps — maybe students in certain groups feel less safe, or staff feel unsupported. Then I set concrete actions and re-measure. Climate improves through consistent attention and follow-through, not a single event or poster campaign.\"\n\nTip: Survey, act on specific gaps, re-measure, follow through.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do if a climate survey has bad results?","Own it honestly with the community, dig into the specifics, co-create a few concrete actions, and re-survey to check progress."]}],
["Technology", "How do you lead technology use and digital equity in your school?", "**Why they ask:** technology leadership and equity are modern expectations.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure technology serves learning goals rather than being bought for its own sake, support teachers with training so devices actually improve instruction, and address digital equity — access to devices and internet at home — because gaps there widen other gaps. I set clear norms for responsible use and data privacy, protect against distraction, and evaluate whether tools deliver results. Technology is a tool for good teaching, with equity and privacy handled deliberately.\"\n\nTip: Tech serves learning; address access equity and data privacy deliberately.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle students without internet at home?","Provide hotspots or devices, offer offline options and in-school access, and partner with the community — I don't assign work that penalizes the gap."]}],
["Morale", "How do you rebuild staff morale after a difficult year?", "**Why they ask:** morale affects retention and performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start by genuinely listening and acknowledging what was hard rather than papering over it, then protect teachers from unnecessary burdens, remove a few real pain points quickly to show I heard them, and recognize good work specifically and often. I give staff voice in decisions, rebuild trust through consistency, and keep perspective on what matters. Morale recovers when teachers feel seen, supported and trusted — which is mostly within a principal's control.\"\n\nTip: Listen, remove real pain points, recognize work, rebuild trust consistently.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's a quick win that rebuilds trust?","Eliminating a pointless meeting or form that wastes teachers' time — it signals you respect them and will act on what you hear."]}],
["Conflict", "Two staff members are in a serious conflict affecting the team. What do you do?", "**Why they ask:** staff conflict resolution is a frequent leadership task.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I address it rather than hoping it resolves, since it affects the team and students. I meet each privately to understand it, look for the root cause — often unclear roles, a misunderstanding, or a real grievance — and then, if appropriate, facilitate a direct conversation with ground rules focused on moving forward professionally. I set clear expectations about conduct, document if needed, and follow up. I stay neutral and fair, and if it's a conduct violation I handle it through the proper process.\"\n\nTip: Understand privately, facilitate directly, set expectations, follow up.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What if one person is clearly in the wrong?","I address that individually through coaching or the performance process, while still resolving the working relationship for the team's sake."]}],
["Leadership", "As a younger or newer leader, how do you earn credibility with veteran staff?", "**Why they ask:** authority gaps are real and common.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I lead with humility and listening first — veteran teachers have deep knowledge, and I learn from them rather than assuming I know better. I earn credibility by being competent, consistent and fair, following through on what I say, supporting teachers concretely, and showing I'm in classrooms and understand the work. I'm confident in my decisions but not arrogant. Respect is earned through actions over time, and I don't demand it on day one.\"\n\nTip: Listen first, be competent and consistent, earn respect through actions.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle a respected veteran who challenges you publicly?","Stay calm and professional, hear the substance, address it privately afterward, and don't get defensive — overreacting costs more credibility than the challenge."]}],
["Programs", "How do you decide whether to keep, cut, or change a program?", "**Why they ask:** stewardship of limited resources requires hard calls.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I evaluate programs against outcomes and our priorities, not sentiment or sunk cost — is it actually improving student results, and is it the best use of those dollars and staff time? I gather data, get input from those closest to it, and consider equity and unintended effects. If it's not working I'm willing to cut or redesign it, communicating the why transparently. Protecting a beloved but ineffective program at students' expense isn't leadership.\"\n\nTip: Judge against outcomes and priorities, not sunk cost or sentiment.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you cut a popular but ineffective program?","Share the outcome data honestly, acknowledge the attachment, explain the trade-off for students, and redirect the resources to what works."]}],
["Accountability", "Tell me about a mistake you made as a leader and how you handled it.", "**Why they ask:** humility and accountability build trust.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I once rolled out a new initiative too fast without enough teacher input, and it landed poorly — staff felt it was done to them. I owned it publicly, paused, gathered their input, and relaunched it collaboratively, which worked far better. I learned that the pace and process of change matter as much as the idea, and that admitting a misstep and correcting it builds more trust than pretending I was right. Modeling accountability sets the tone for the whole staff.\"\n\nTip: Own it, correct it, and name what you learned.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why admit a mistake publicly rather than quietly fix it?","Modeling accountability builds trust and a culture where staff also own mistakes and improve, rather than hiding them."]}],
["Recognition", "How do you recognize and celebrate staff and student success?", "**Why they ask:** recognition sustains culture and motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make recognition specific, genuine and frequent rather than generic — naming exactly what a teacher did well means far more than a blanket thank-you. I celebrate both big wins and quiet everyday excellence, highlight student growth not just top performers, and create a culture where staff appreciate each other too. It costs little and does a lot for morale and retention. I'm careful it's authentic, because hollow or unequal praise does more harm than none.\"\n\nTip: Specific, genuine, frequent recognition of effort and growth.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["Why does specific praise matter more than general praise?","Specific praise tells people exactly what to keep doing and shows you actually noticed — generic praise feels hollow and changes nothing."]}],
["Time", "How do you protect time for instructional leadership amid constant operational demands?", "**Why they ask:** principals are easily consumed by management minutiae.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The urgent operational work — buses, discipline, email, facilities — will eat every minute if I let it, so I protect it deliberately. I schedule classroom time and keep it sacred, delegate operational tasks to a strong team, batch and systematize routine work, and triage so I'm spending my own time on the highest-leverage things only I can do. Being visible in classrooms and leading instruction is the job; the rest is necessary but can't crowd it out.\"\n\nTip: Protect classroom time, delegate operations, triage ruthlessly.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What do you delegate first?","Operational and routine tasks a capable team member can own — I keep instructional leadership and the decisions only I can make."]}],
["Data", "How do you lead data conversations with your staff?", "**Why they ask:** data leadership drives improvement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make data use safe and purposeful — it's for learning and improving, not blaming teachers — and I keep it focused on a few meaningful measures rather than drowning people. In team meetings we look at student work and assessment results together, ask what it tells us and what we'll do differently, and then actually do it and check back. I model curiosity over judgment and make sure teachers have time and support to act. Data without action is just anxiety.\"\n\nTip: Safe, focused, action-oriented data conversations — not blame.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you keep data from feeling like a gotcha?","Frame it as collective problem-solving, protect it from evaluation, and focus on what we'll do next rather than who's at fault."]}],
["Equity", "How do you ensure discipline and opportunities are equitable across student groups?", "**Why they ask:** disproportionality is a serious equity and legal issue.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I monitor data by subgroup — discipline referrals and suspensions, access to advanced courses and gifted programs, graduation and course outcomes — because disproportionality is often invisible until you disaggregate. Where I find gaps, I dig into causes like inconsistent discipline, bias, or access barriers, and address them through staff training, clearer practices, and deliberate access. I hold the whole staff accountable for every group, and I treat equity as concrete practice and data, not just a statement of values.\"\n\nTip: Disaggregate data, find causes, change practice — equity is measurable.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why might discipline data reveal bias?","If one group is suspended far more for subjective offenses like 'defiance,' that pattern signals inconsistent or biased application worth examining and fixing."]}],
["Finance", "How do you advocate for resources for your school?", "**Why they ask:** principals must secure what their students need.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make the case with data and student impact, not just need — showing specifically what a resource would change for students — and I build relationships with central office so I'm a credible, reasonable partner rather than only ever asking. I pursue grants and partnerships to supplement, prioritize ruthlessly when funds are limited, and am transparent with staff about constraints. I advocate persistently but professionally, understanding the district's broader constraints while fighting for my school.\"\n\nTip: Make a data- and impact-based case; build credibility with central office.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What if your resource request is denied?","I ask what would change the answer, look for grants or partnerships, reprioritize within what I have, and keep making the case over time."]}],
["Sustainability", "How do you sustain an improvement effort over multiple years?", "**Why they ask:** real school improvement is a long game, and most efforts fizzle.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I protect focus — resisting the urge to pile on new initiatives — and build the work into systems, roles and the schedule so it doesn't depend on my energy alone. I celebrate progress to maintain momentum, monitor data to adjust, and develop staff capacity and leadership so the work is owned broadly. I communicate the 'why' repeatedly. Sustained improvement comes from consistency and depth over years, not a flurry of new programs each fall.\"\n\nTip: Protect focus, build into systems, distribute ownership, stay consistent.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why do most improvement efforts fail to stick?","Initiative overload and dependence on one leader — too many priorities and no systems mean the work evaporates when attention or that leader moves on."]}],
["Student voice", "How do you incorporate student voice into decisions?", "**Why they ask:** student voice improves decisions and engagement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I create real channels — advisory groups, surveys, listening sessions — and I act on what students say and tell them what came of it, because tokenistic input they never see honored breeds cynicism. Students have sharp insights into climate, teaching and what helps them learn. I weigh their voice alongside other factors in decisions about things that affect them, and I use it especially to understand the experience of students whose perspectives adults often miss.\"\n\nTip: Create real channels, act on input, and close the loop with students.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you avoid student voice being tokenistic?","Give them influence on real decisions, act visibly on their input, and report back what changed — otherwise it's just for show."]}],
["Legal", "How do you handle student records and privacy (FERPA) at the school level?", "**Why they ask:** principals are responsible for compliance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure student records are accessed and shared only on a legitimate educational need-to-know basis, that staff understand FERPA and are trained, and that records are stored and transmitted securely. I'm careful with sensitive information, honor parents' and eligible students' access rights, and have clear procedures for records requests. Privacy failures erode trust and create legal risk, so I set the expectation and the systems that keep us compliant without making it bureaucratic for legitimate uses.\"\n\nTip: Need-to-know access, staff training, secure handling, honor access rights.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Can teachers discuss a student's details in the staff lounge?","Only on a need-to-know basis for serving the student — casual discussion where others can overhear risks violating privacy."]}],
["Scenario", "A parent threatens legal action over a disciplinary decision. How do you respond?", "**Why they ask:** composure and legal awareness under pressure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I stay calm and professional, don't get defensive or make promises I can't keep, and make sure my decision and process were fair, consistent and documented. I listen to the parent's concern genuinely and explain the reasoning and their options, including any appeal process. I loop in my supervisor and, where appropriate, district legal counsel early, and I follow their guidance. Solid process and documentation, plus honest respectful communication, are the best protection — and often defuse the threat.\"\n\nTip: Stay calm, rely on fair documented process, involve district/legal early.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why involve the district or legal early?","So I follow proper guidance, don't say something that creates liability, and ensure the response is consistent with policy and law."]}],
["Instruction", "What does high-quality instruction look like to you, and how do you promote it?", "**Why they ask:** the instructional core is the heart of the job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"High-quality instruction has clear objectives, students doing the cognitive work rather than passively receiving, checks for understanding that drive adjustment, rigor with support so all students access grade-level content, and strong relationships. I promote it by naming a shared vision of good teaching, giving frequent coaching feedback, providing aligned PD and collaboration time, and celebrating and spreading great practice. I keep the whole system — schedule, hiring, PD — pointed at improving what happens in classrooms.\"\n\nTip: Students doing the thinking, rigor with support, checks that drive adjustment.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you spread great teaching from one classroom to others?","Make it visible — peer observation, teachers sharing in teams, and structured opportunities to learn from the strongest practitioners on staff."]}],
["Wellbeing", "How do you take care of yourself so you can lead sustainably?", "**Why they ask:** principal burnout is high and affects the whole school.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I know the job is relentless and that a depleted leader helps no one, so I build a strong team and delegate real responsibility, set boundaries on when I'm reachable, and protect some time outside work. I lean on a network of other principals for support and perspective, keep connected to why I do this through the student and staff wins, and try not to let perfectionism make every problem mine to solve instantly. Sustainability is a leadership skill, not a luxury.\"\n\nTip: Build a team, set boundaries, lean on peers, resist perfectionism.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the first boundary you set?","Not being reachable at all hours — I delegate and trust my team so true emergencies reach me, but the job doesn't run 24/7."]}],
["Scenario", "Student test scores dropped this year. How do you analyze and respond?", "**Why they ask:** data analysis and non-defensive problem-solving.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd resist both panic and excuses and dig into the data — which students, subgroups, subjects and standards, and whether it's a real trend or noise, a cohort difference, or a change like staffing or attendance. I'd involve teachers in interpreting it, identify the controllable causes, and target a few high-leverage responses rather than overreacting with a dozen new programs. Then I'd monitor leading indicators through the year instead of waiting for next year's scores. One year of data is a signal to investigate, not a verdict to flail at.\"\n\nTip: Investigate causes carefully, target controllable factors, monitor leading indicators.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you keep teachers from feeling blamed for a score drop?","Frame it as collective problem-solving focused on what we can control, and look at causes like attendance and systems, not just teaching."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want to lead this school specifically?", "**Why they ask:** fit and genuine commitment to this community.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd ground it in specifics I've learned about this school — its community, strengths, challenges and where I believe I can help — and connect them to my skills and values. I'd be honest that I'm drawn to the work this particular community needs, not just any principalship, and that I want to invest here for years, because real improvement takes time and trust. I'd share what I'd listen for and prioritize in my first months, showing I've thought seriously about this place.\"\n\nTip: Tie genuine commitment to specific, researched reasons for this school.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What would you prioritize in your first year here?","Listening and building trust, understanding the data and people, and identifying a few high-leverage priorities before making big changes."]}]
]);

// School Principal / Administrator — part 3. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["ed-principal"] = (QB["ed-principal"] || []).concat([
["Development", "How do you support and retain new teachers?", "**Why they ask:** new-teacher attrition is costly and often preventable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"New teachers are most at risk of leaving, so I invest in a real induction: a strong mentor, protected support, realistic early expectations, and frequent low-stakes feedback rather than only the formal evaluation. I shield them from being handed the hardest assignments and every extra duty, check in on their wellbeing, and make it safe to ask for help. I'd rather spend heavily to keep a promising new teacher than lose them and rehire. Good induction turns survivors into strong veterans.\"\n\nTip: Real mentoring, realistic early load, frequent support — not sink-or-swim.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not give new teachers the same load as veterans?","They're still building core skills; piling on the toughest classes and extra duties is how promising new teachers burn out and quit."]}],
["Community", "How do you partner with your parent organization (PTA/PTO) and family leaders?", "**Why they ask:** parent-leadership relationships affect support and climate.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat the parent organization as a genuine partner — communicating openly, being present at their events, valuing their input and fundraising, and aligning their energy with the school's priorities rather than letting it pull in competing directions. I'm honest about what I can and can't do, keep the relationship respectful even in disagreement, and make sure family engagement reaches beyond the already-involved families to those we hear from least. Strong, trusting family leadership amplifies everything the school is trying to do.\"\n\nTip: Genuine partnership, aligned priorities, and reach beyond the usual families.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle a parent organization that oversteps into school operations?","Respectfully clarify roles — they're valued partners, but staffing and instructional decisions are the school's — while keeping the relationship collaborative."]}],
["Crisis", "How do you plan for emergency reunification and family communication?", "**Why they ask:** reunification is a critical, often-overlooked part of emergency planning.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure we have a clear, practiced reunification plan — how students are accounted for, released only to authorized adults with verification, and where families go — because a chaotic reunification can undo a good emergency response. I coordinate with first responders and the district, keep family contact information current, and plan accurate, timely communication through official channels to prevent panic and rumor. We practice it, not just write it, because under stress people fall back on what they've rehearsed.\"\n\nTip: A practiced, verified reunification plan with clear official communication.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is controlled reunification so important?","Releasing students safely and only to authorized adults prevents dangerous chaos — a disorganized reunification can create new harm after the initial emergency."]}]
]);
